:: The Lord's Prayer (Our Father) in Irish [prayer regular] {"position":"500,275","size":"100,100"}
<h1>The Lord's Prayer (Our Father) in Irish</h1>

<p>On this page you will hear a recitation of the Lord's Prayer by Tomás Ó Dálaigh from Co. Kerry. It was recorded primarily for phonetic purposes. There are five examples of each of these prayers in the Doegen collection. You are invited to copy it and record yourself reciting the prayer in your own voice.</p>

<p>If you learned the Ár nAthair in school, you may find that the version recited here includes some archaic or regional differences to the version with which you are familiar. You are welcome to record either version.</p>

<p>This recording comes from the Tionscadal Gréasáin Cheirníní Doegen, The Doegen Records Web Project, wich is an archive of Irish dialect sound recordings made during 1928-31. It contains folktales, songs and other material recited by native Irish speakers from 17 counties. Crucially, it includes examples of dialects that are now extinct.</p>

<p>In 1926 the Irish government asked Dr Doegen to make recordings of Irish speech in the Gaeltacht and in areas of the country where Irish had suffered decline. The Department of Education asked the Royal Irish Academy if it would organize such a scheme (Survey of Dialects) and the Academy’s Irish Studies Committee undertook the task. The recordings were made by Dr Doegen and his assistant Karl Tempel between 1928 and 1931.</p>


''Source:''
<p>Royal Irish Academy &#91;Depositor&#93;. Paidir an Tiarna; an tÁivé Máiria.The Lord's prayer; the Ave Maria. Dr Wilhelm Doegen. (1928-09-13). Sound &#91;Type&#93;. Digital Repository of Ireland (2022) &#91;Publisher&#93;. <a href="https://doi.org/10.7486/DRI.d7926464h" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.7486/DRI.d7926464h</a></p>

<audio id="audioClip" preload="auto" controls>
<source src="https://digital-repository-of-ireland.github.io/SpeakLikeMe/assets/arnathair.mp3">
</audio>

[[Let me try!|recordlordsprayer]]

<p>The background image on each of these pages is Hunt Museum, Limerick &#91;Depositor&#93;. Reliquary cross-pendant . Spanish-English. (c.1500 - c1550). Image &#91;Type&#93;. Digital Repository of Ireland (2022) &#91;Publisher&#93. <a href="https://doi.org/10.7486/DRI.bn99q0259-2" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.7486/DRI.bn99q0259-2</a></p>




