<p>If you learned the Ár nAthair in school, you may find that the version recited here includes some archaic or regional differences to the version with which you are familiar. You are welcome to record either version</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Transcription</th><th>Translation</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ár nAthair atá ar neamh</td><td>Our father, who art in heaven,</td></tr>
<tr><td>Go naomhaítear t'ainm,</td><td>Hallowed be thy name,</td></tr>
<tr><td>Go dtaga do ríocht,</td><td>Thy kingdom come,</td></tr>
<tr><td>Go ndéantar do thoil</td><td>Thy will be done,</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ar an dtalamh mar a déantar ar neamh.</td><td>On earth as it is in heaven.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ár n-arán laethúil tabhair dúinn inniubh.</td><td>Give us today our daily bread.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maith dhúinn ár gcionta</td><td>Forgive us our trespasses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fé mar mhaithimid do chách a chiontaíos inár n-aghaidh.</td><td>As we forgive those who trespass against us.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ná leig sinn i gcathuíbh ach saor sinn ó olc.</td><td>Lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Amen.</td><td>Amen.</td></tr>
</table>

